**Put comments in their own collection keyed by `postId`, with an index on `{ postId: 1, createdAt: -1 }`, keep a denormalized comment count on the post, and optionally embed the latest few comments for the first page. Embedding all comments fails for popular posts because the array is unbounded.**

**Why not embed everything:** comment counts are unbounded, concurrent comment writes all contend on the same post document, and loading the post would pull thousands of comments you do not display.

```js
// posts
{ _id: ObjectId("..."), title: "Hello", commentCount: 1284,
  recentComments: [ { _id: ..., author: { id: 7, name: "Ana" }, text: "Nice", createdAt: ... } ] }

// comments
{ _id: ObjectId("..."), postId: ObjectId("..."), parentId: null,
  author: { id: 7, name: "Ana" }, text: "Nice", createdAt: ISODate("2026-10-01") }

db.comments.createIndex({ postId: 1, createdAt: -1, _id: -1 });

// keyset pagination: next page after the last comment seen
db.comments.find({ postId: p, $or: [
  { createdAt: { $lt: lastTs } }, { createdAt: lastTs, _id: { $lt: lastId } } ] })
  .sort({ createdAt: -1, _id: -1 }).limit(20);
```

**Writing a comment:** insert into `comments`, then `$inc` the post's `commentCount` and `$push` with `$slice: -10` into `recentComments`. If the two must agree exactly, use a transaction; usually a slightly stale count is acceptable and a periodic job reconciles it.

**Author names:** store `author.name` as an extended reference so listing comments needs no `$lookup`; when a user renames, update their comments in the background.

**Nested replies:**
- **Parent reference** (`parentId`) for one or two levels, loading replies per thread.
- **Materialized path** (`path: "c1/c7/c19"`) or an `ancestors` array for deep threads, so a single indexed query fetches a whole subtree.

**Use the schema versioning pattern: add a `schemaVersion` field, make the app read both old and new shapes, write the new shape, and migrate old documents lazily on read or with a throttled background job. Back it with `$jsonSchema` validation so bad shapes cannot creep in.**

**Why it is still a migration:** MongoDB does not enforce one shape per collection, so documents of different versions coexist. That flexibility avoids downtime, but every reader must handle every live version until old ones are gone.

**Steps for a change such as splitting `name` into `firstName`/`lastName`:**
1. Deploy code that reads both shapes and writes the new one, setting `schemaVersion: 2`.
2. Optionally migrate on read: when a v1 document is loaded, upgrade and save it.
3. Backfill the rest in batches by `_id` range, with pauses and a check on replication lag.
4. When no v1 documents remain, remove the compatibility code and tighten validation.

```js
// batch backfill
let last = MinKey;
while (true) {
  const batch = db.users.find({ _id: { $gt: last }, schemaVersion: { $ne: 2 } })
    .sort({ _id: 1 }).limit(1000).toArray();
  if (!batch.length) break;
  db.users.bulkWrite(batch.map(u => ({ updateOne: {
    filter: { _id: u._id, schemaVersion: { $ne: 2 } },
    update: [{ $set: { firstName: { $arrayElemAt: [{ $split: ["$name", " "] }, 0] },
                       schemaVersion: 2 } }] } })));
  last = batch.at(-1)._id;
}
```

**Validation:** add a `$jsonSchema` validator with `validationLevel: "moderate"` (only checks inserts and documents that already match) and `validationAction: "warn"` first, watch the logs, then switch to `error`.

**Trade-offs:** lazy migration spreads load but leaves a long tail of old documents and permanent compatibility code; an eager backfill finishes cleanly but must be throttled. Index changes for new fields need their own rolling build.

**ESR is MongoDB's guideline for compound index field order: Equality fields first, then Sort fields, then Range fields. That order lets the index jump to the exact equality prefix, return documents already sorted, and still bound the scan with the range.**

**Why this order:**
- **Equality** fields narrow to one contiguous section of the index.
- **Sort** fields next mean entries within that section are already in the requested order, so there is no blocking in-memory `SORT` stage (which has a 100 MB memory limit before spilling to disk).
- **Range** fields last are checked while walking the sorted entries. If the range came before the sort field, the matching entries would be ordered by the range field, not the sort field, and MongoDB would have to sort them.

```js
// query
db.orders.find({ status: "shipped", total: { $gt: 100 } })
         .sort({ createdAt: -1 }).limit(20);

// E, S, R
db.orders.createIndex({ status: 1, createdAt: -1, total: 1 });

// worse: E, R, S -> SORT stage in the plan
db.orders.createIndex({ status: 1, total: 1, createdAt: -1 });
```

**Details interviewers probe:**
- An equality match on `$in` with a few values behaves like equality for filtering, but combined with a sort it becomes a merge of several sorted streams (`SORT_MERGE`); with many values treat it like a range.
- `$ne`, `$nin` and regex without an anchored prefix are effectively ranges or full scans.
- With a `limit`, ESR means the query can stop after 20 index entries even if millions match.

**Check it:** `explain("executionStats")` should show `IXSCAN` with no `SORT` stage and `totalKeysExamined` close to `nReturned`.

**A covered query is answered entirely from an index: every filtered field and every returned field is in the index, so MongoDB never fetches the documents. In `explain` you see `totalDocsExamined: 0` and no `FETCH` stage.**

**Requirements:**
- All fields in the filter are in the index.
- All fields in the projection are in the index.
- `_id` is excluded from the projection (it is returned by default) unless it is part of the index.
- No multikey index on the projected array field, and no fields inside embedded documents that the index does not hold exactly.

```js
db.users.createIndex({ email: 1, status: 1, name: 1 });

db.users.find(
  { email: "ana@example.com" },
  { _id: 0, status: 1, name: 1 }
).explain("executionStats");
// winningPlan: PROJECTION_COVERED <- IXSCAN
// totalDocsExamined: 0
```

**Why it is fast:** indexes are smaller than documents and more likely to be in the WiredTiger cache, so covered reads avoid random document fetches and decompression.

**When to use:** hot lookups that need two or three fields (login checks, autocomplete, existence checks), and counts over an indexed range.

**Trade-offs:** adding fields to an index just for coverage makes it larger and every write slower. Cover only a few high-volume queries. Unlike Postgres, there is no separate visibility check, so a covered query really does skip the documents.

**Use one when an invariant spans several documents or collections and partial completion would be wrong, such as moving money between two accounts or creating an order while reserving stock. Prefer modelling so that related data lives in one document, because single-document writes are already atomic and much cheaper.**

**Good uses:**
- Transfers and double-entry ledgers touching two account documents.
- Creating an order and decrementing inventory in a separate collection.
- Writing a business record plus an outbox event atomically.

**How they work:** snapshot isolation over a replica set or sharded cluster since 4.0/4.2. Writes are not visible to others until commit. Concurrent writers to the same document produce write conflicts; one transaction aborts and must retry.

```js
const session = client.startSession();
try {
  await session.withTransaction(async () => {
    await accounts.updateOne({ _id: from, balance: { $gte: 100 } },
                             { $inc: { balance: -100 } }, { session });
    await accounts.updateOne({ _id: to }, { $inc: { balance: 100 } }, { session });
    await ledger.insertOne({ from, to, amount: 100, at: new Date() }, { session });
  }, { writeConcern: { w: "majority" }, readConcern: { level: "snapshot" } });
} finally {
  await session.endSession();
}
```

`withTransaction` retries the whole callback on `TransientTransactionError` and retries the commit on `UnknownTransactionCommitResult`, so the callback must be safe to rerun (no side effects like sending emails inside it). Also check the first update matched a document and throw to abort if not.

**Limits and costs:**
- Default 60 s lifetime (`transactionLifetimeLimitSeconds`); keep them short and small (well under 1,000 documents).
- Cross-shard transactions use two-phase commit and add latency.
- Long transactions pin WiredTiger cache history and increase pressure.

**Rule:** if most of your writes need transactions, the schema is probably modelled relationally; revisit embedding.

**Write the business change and an event document into an `outbox` collection in the same transaction, then a separate relay process reads the outbox and publishes to the broker, marking events as sent. This removes the dual-write problem: the event exists if and only if the change committed.**

**The problem it solves:** "save order, then publish OrderCreated" has two independent writes. If the process crashes between them, the order exists but no event was sent; if you publish first and the save fails, consumers act on an order that does not exist.

```js
await session.withTransaction(async () => {
  await orders.insertOne(order, { session });
  await outbox.insertOne({ _id: new ObjectId(), type: "OrderCreated",
    aggregateId: order._id, payload: { total: order.total },
    createdAt: new Date(), sentAt: null }, { session });
});

// relay: tail with a change stream (resumable), publish, then mark sent
const cs = outbox.watch([{ $match: { operationType: "insert" } }], { resumeAfter: savedToken });
for await (const ev of cs) {
  await producer.send({ topic: "orders", key: String(ev.fullDocument.aggregateId),
                        messages: [{ value: JSON.stringify(ev.fullDocument) }] });
  await outbox.updateOne({ _id: ev.documentKey._id }, { $set: { sentAt: new Date() } });
  await saveToken(ev._id);
}
```

**Relay options:**
- **Polling** `{ sentAt: null }` with an index: simple and robust.
- **Change streams:** low latency and resumable with a stored resume token.
- **Kafka Connect MongoDB source connector or Debezium:** CDC without custom code.

**Delivery semantics:** the relay can crash after publishing but before marking sent, so events can be sent twice. Consumers dedupe by event `_id` (a processed-events collection with a unique index) or apply idempotent updates.

**Ordering:** use the aggregate id as the partition key so events for one entity stay in order. Clean old sent events with a TTL index on `sentAt`.

**Change streams let an application subscribe to inserts, updates, deletes and other changes on a collection, database or whole cluster in real time, built on the replication oplog. They are resumable with a token, ordered, and only show majority-committed changes, which makes them a safe base for event-driven features.**

**Typical uses:**
- Syncing a search index, cache or analytics store (CDC).
- Outbox relays to Kafka.
- Real-time UI updates (notifications, dashboards).
- Triggers, audit logs and cross-service integration.

```js
const pipeline = [{ $match: { operationType: { $in: ["insert", "update"] },
                              "fullDocument.status": "paid" } }];
const stream = db.collection("orders").watch(pipeline, {
  fullDocument: "updateLookup",
  resumeAfter: await loadToken(),
});
for await (const change of stream) {
  await indexInSearch(change.fullDocument);   // idempotent upsert
  await saveToken(change._id);
}
```

**Mechanics to know:**
- **Resume tokens:** each event's `_id`. Store it after processing and pass `resumeAfter` or `startAfter` on restart.
- **Oplog window:** resume only works if the token is still in the oplog. If the consumer is down longer than the window, the stream fails and you need a full resync; size the oplog accordingly.
- **Full documents:** updates include only the delta by default. `fullDocument: "updateLookup"` fetches the current document (which may be newer than the change). Pre- and post-images (`changeStreamPreAndPostImages`) give exact before/after versions.
- **Sharded clusters:** open the stream through `mongos`; it merges per-shard streams into one globally ordered stream.

**Trade-offs:** each open stream costs server resources, so prefer one consumer per purpose rather than one per user connection, and fan out from your own service. Delivery is at-least-once from your application's perspective, so make handlers idempotent.

**A TTL index automatically deletes documents once a date field is older than a set number of seconds, ideal for sessions, tokens and logs. A partial index only indexes documents matching a filter, which keeps it small and enables things like "unique only among active documents".**

**TTL indexes:**

```js
// delete sessions 30 minutes after lastSeen
db.sessions.createIndex({ lastSeen: 1 }, { expireAfterSeconds: 1800 });

// expire at an exact per-document time
db.tokens.createIndex({ expiresAt: 1 }, { expireAfterSeconds: 0 });
```

- A background monitor runs roughly every 60 seconds, so deletion is not instant. Your queries should still filter out expired documents if exactness matters.
- Deletes are normal writes: they replicate, cost I/O, and a burst of expiries can load the cluster. Spread expiry times if possible.
- The field must be a date (or an array of dates). TTL works on time series collections with `expireAfterSeconds` at the collection level.

**Partial indexes:**

```js
// unique email only among non-deleted users (soft delete)
db.users.createIndex({ email: 1 },
  { unique: true, partialFilterExpression: { deletedAt: null } });

// index only the small set of open tickets
db.tickets.createIndex({ assignee: 1, createdAt: -1 },
  { partialFilterExpression: { status: "open" } });
```

- Smaller, faster indexes and cheaper writes for documents outside the filter.
- The query must include a condition implying the filter (e.g. `status: "open"`), otherwise the planner cannot use the index.
- They replace older **sparse** indexes, which only skip documents missing the field.

**Real-world combo:** a partial TTL index can delete only unverified sign-up records after 24 hours while keeping verified ones.
